leap71/PicoGKRuntime @ 0f26321c (26.2): render lattices through openvdb's
parallel tube-complex voxelizer instead of the serial dense-accessor loop.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of PicoGK (not affiliated with or endorsed by LEAP 71), ships this algorithm as
a separate export (src/pico-lattice.cpp -> Voxels_RenderLatticeTubes), its
default lattice renderer, with `createPico({ serialLattice: true })` selecting
the original loop. This file is the same algorithm shaped as a diff behind the
existing Voxels_RenderLattice entry point.

PREREQUISITE: picovoxel's patches/PicoGKRuntime/0003-lattice-parameter-
accessors.patch, seven `inline ... const` getters on LatticeSphere/LatticeBeam
(vecCenter, fRadius, vecStart, vecEnd, fRadStart, fRadEnd, bRoundCap). It only
inserts code and changes no declaration. This diff applies to a pristine tree
but compiles only with those accessors: without them fSdValue() is the only
readable surface on a beam, which forces every renderer into the per-sample
shape this patch replaces.

WHY. Voxels::RenderLattice walks every sphere and every beam in turn and, for
each, walks its padded bounding box voxel by voxel through one
FloatGrid::Accessor, taking min(element SDF, existing value): one thread, one
element at a time. Lattices reach hundreds of thousands of elements
(LEAP71_HelixHeatX renders 1,197,460 beams across 37 lattices). openvdb has
shipped the parallel replacement since 12.1: tools::createLevelSetTubeComplex
(LevelSetTubes.h, already #included by this file at line 49 and unused) takes
vertex, segment and radius arrays and voxelizes them with a tbb reduce over
spatially bucketed segments; each bucket drives ConvexVoxelizer's scanline walk
over only the voxels the shape touches, and per-thread trees are combined with
a node-stealing csg union.

The mapping is exact: a round-capped PicoGK beam is the convex hull of its two
end spheres, and so is a tapered capsule. The full case table, including the
two cases that do not map, is in the doc comment on bRenderLatticeTubes() below.

MEASURED, WebAssembly (picovoxel, Apple M2 Pro, 12 threads, 2026-07-26; paired
and order-alternated, 12 repeats, bootstrap CI on the median log ratio):

  - HelixHeatX's two largest creation stages together (355.6k + 65k beams):
    8380.4 -> 1384.9 ms = 6.05x at 1.0 mm, and 13.2x at 0.5 mm;
  - single-threaded, the algorithm alone is 2.57-4.23x faster: the scanline
    voxelizer beats the dense bounding-box walk before any thread is added;
  - flat-capped lattices (the serial fallback) time at 1.00x;
  - memory: +61.6 MiB peak at 10^5 beams (112.8 against 51.2 MiB heap growth at
    0.25 mm), the same single- and multi-threaded.

MEASURED, native (picovoxel's bench/native-heatx harness on the C# HelixHeatX
fixture, Apple M2 Pro, .NET 9.0.16, 2026-08-09): the whole fixture wall,
previews and STL export included, is 2.53x faster (geometric mean over six
voxel sizes, 2.83x at 1.0 mm to 2.22x at 0.5 mm, median of 5 runs per size)
than the same runtime build without this patch. Raw runs:
bench/results/native/native-heatx-{patched,u5}-2026-08-09.jsonl. STL volume
agrees within 2.6e-5 relative at every size, with identical bounds.

DETERMINISM. createLevelSetTubeComplex drives the voxelizer under
tbb::parallel_reduce with the auto partitioner, so the csg join tree's shape
depends on how many workers turn up. csg union is a min, so the values do not
depend on order, but the tree topology does. This patch drives the same
voxelizer under tbb::parallel_deterministic_reduce with a grain size derived
only from the bucket count, so the split tree is a function of the input alone.
Measured: 3/3 runs identical and 1 thread identical to 12 (level-set volume as
hex double, mesh counts, FNV-1a over the vertex and triangle bytes) on five
fixtures at 0.5 mm, at no measurable cost.

OUTPUT VALUES change slightly against the serial loop. The two paths build the
same shapes through different narrow-band constructions. Across 17 comparison
fixtures at 0.5 mm: volume and area within 3%, bounds delta 0, narrow-band
distance change max 9.8e-6 mm and mean 1.3e-7 mm, and volume moved at most
2.3e-5 relative on picovoxel's pinned models. Several cases (sphere, tapered
beam, zero-length round beam, every flat-capped fixture) are bit-exact. A
byte-pinned corpus moves once, by about that much.

tools::checkLevelSet: the serial output is not clean where two elements overlap
(the per-element min leaves gradient violations: 458,402 voxels on one fin
lattice); the tube-complex result reports the identical diagnosis everywhere
except nested end spheres, where it is clean and the serial output has 160
violations.

NESTED END SPHERES. When a beam's end spheres nest, (p0-p1)^2 <= (r0-r1)^2,
fSdvRoundCone takes sqrtf of a negative product. For r 6 -> 1 the serial loop
renders -90.7% of the true volume; the tube complex renders -0.37%. The
separate fix picogkruntime-roundcone-nested-spheres.patch corrects the serial
path; if it lands first, the two paths agree on this case as well.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. Voxels_RenderLattice keeps its signature and its
semantics (union into the existing grid), so the C# binding needs no change. No
new dependency: LevelSetTubes.h is already included, and Prune.h and the two
tbb headers already arrive through Composite.h; they are spelled out so the
file states what it uses.

SHAPE. RenderLattice gains a five-line dispatch at its head and keeps its serial
body verbatim as the fallback; the new work is one private method. The dispatch
recurses at most once: the flat-capped remainder is authored with
bRoundCap = false and holds no spheres, so the second call takes the serial
branch.

COMPOSITION with picovoxel's patches/PicoGKRuntime series (checked with
`patch -p1 --fuzz=0` and a Release build of the composed tree):

  - 0003 lattice parameter accessors: required (see above);
  - 0004 lattice beam value storage: optional. This patch reads beams through
    two oBeamRef overloads, one per storage form, so it compiles on either;
  - 0001 parallel mesh flatten and 0002 post-fill prune: no interaction. The
    tube path returns before 0002's PruneFill(); the complex is pruned on
    construction and csgUnion prunes on exit.

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree, and
onto that tree with patches/PicoGKRuntime/0001-0004 applied.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

